course: chinese-street
lesson: 7
title: Counting to 100 and lucky numbers
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Big numbers in Mandarin are built like Lego. Eleven is shí yī, «ten-one»; twelve is shí'èr, «ten-two»; twenty is èrshí, «two-tens». There is no «eleven» or «twelve» to learn by heart.
explain: Order matters: a number before shí says how many tens, a number after it adds ones. So èrshí is 20, but shí'èr is 12 — swap them in a shop and you pay the wrong price.
explain: Numbers also make jokes and wishes: 520 (wǔ èr líng) sounds a bit like «I love you», 88 (bābā) is «bye-bye», and 99 sounds like «for ever and ever».
explain: When you want to know how many, ask jǐ ge? for small numbers and duōshao? for big ones or prices. And in a race, the shout is «wǒ dì yī!» (I'm first!).
fact: líng («ling?», rising) is zero, used in scores like líng bǐ sān (nil-three) and inside numbers like yìbǎi líng wǔ (105).
fact: shíyī («SHR-ee») is eleven, ten-one; shí'èr («SHR-ar») is twelve, ten-two.
fact: èrshí («AR-shr») is twenty, two-tens; sānshí («SAHN-shr») is thirty, and in fast speech the shí in tens is said lighter.
fact: sìshísì («SZ-shr-SZ») is forty-four, the star of the tongue-twister sì shì sì, shí shì shí, shísì shì shísì, sìshí shì sìshí; to some grandparents it is doubly unlucky.
fact: jiǔshíjiǔ («JYO-shr-JYO») is ninety-nine; it sounds like «long, long time» and appears in wishes for lasting friendship.
fact: yìbǎi («ee-BYE») is one hundred; yī becomes yì before the dipping bǎi, and yìbǎi fēn is full marks.
fact: duōshao? («DWAW-shaow») means how many or how much, for bigger or unknown amounts: duōshao qián? is how much money?
fact: jǐ ge? («JEE guh») means how many, for small numbers; without a question it means a few: jǐ ge rén = a few people.
fact: dì yī («DEE ee») means first; dì before a number makes it an order, so dì yī míng is first place.
fact: wǔ èr líng (520) sounds a bit like wǒ ài nǐ (I love you), so kids giggle about it, and 20 May is a sweethearts' day online in China; 88 (bābā) means bye-bye.
fact: Prices in Chinese shops often end in 8 or 9 for luck, not only for marketing.
exercise: What is líng? | Zero | Ten | One hundred | Eleven
exercise: A football score of «líng bǐ sān» is | Nil-three | Three-nil | Three-all | Ten-three
exercise: How do you build eleven? | shí yī, ten-one | yī shí, one-ten | shí shí yī | yī yī
exercise: shí'èr is | Twelve | Twenty | Two | Two hundred
exercise: èrshí is | Twenty | Twelve | Two | Eleven
exercise: What is sānshí? | Thirty | Thirteen | Three | Three hundred
exercise: Which number is the star of the «sì shì sì, shí shì shí» tongue-twister? | Forty-four | Fourteen | Four hundred | Ten
exercise: Why does 99 appear in friendship wishes? | jiǔ sounds like «long-lasting» | 99 is the emperor's number | It means «best friends» in English | It is the biggest two-digit number
exercise: Why is it yìbǎi and not yībǎi? | yī becomes yì before a dipping tone | It is a different word for one, only for hundreds | Southern people say it that way | It means one thousand, not one hundred
exercise: At the market you want to know the price. You ask | duōshao qián? | jǐ suì le? | nǐ qù nǎr? | dì yī?
exercise: «nǐ yǒu jǐ ge gēge?» (gēge is big brother) asks | How many big brothers have you got? | How old is your big brother now? | Where is your big brother today? | Is your brother first in the class?
exercise: You win the race. What do you shout? | wǒ dì yī! | wǒ yī dì! | wǒ yìbǎi! | wǒ líng!
exercise: What does dì do in front of a number? | Turns counting into order: first, second | Makes the number ten times bigger | Turns the number into a question | Makes it negative, like minus two
exercise: Ming writes 520 next to a classmate's name. What is it? | Teasing about a crush, «I love you» | Her phone number from the class list | Her test mark out of a thousand | Her house number
exercise: What does 88 mean in a chat? | Bye-bye | Very lucky | Eighty-eight points | Hurry up
exercise: How do you say 105? | yìbǎi líng wǔ | yìbǎi wǔ | yī líng wǔ shí | wǔ yìbǎi
exercise: Why do many shop prices end in 8? | 8 is lucky, it rhymes with «get rich» | 8 is the number the tax man wants | 8 looks like a pile of coins | Shops aren't allowed to print 9
test: «12 is èrshí, 2 tens innit» Which is right? | No, 12 is shí'èr; èrshí is 20 | Yes, èrshí is twelve, bang on | Both mean twelve, it's whatever | No, 12 is èr èr
test: What would happen if you paid èrshí kuài for something priced shí'èr kuài? | You'd pay 20 instead of 12 | You'd pay exactly right | You'd pay 2 | You'd pay 200
test: «we lost nil-three, crap» How would Ming say the score? | líng bǐ sān | sān bǐ líng | sān líng | shí sān
test: You ask «jǐ ge?» about the stars in the sky. Why do friends laugh? | jǐ ge is for small numbers; duōshao suits big ones | jǐ ge means «what colour», not «how many» | It's a rude word | You asked how old the stars are, by mistake
test: «my mark was yībǎi fēn» What should you really hear? | yìbǎi fēn, full marks | yībǎi fēn, half marks | yìbǎi líng, zero | shí fēn, ten marks
test: A friend says «jǐ ge rén lái le» without asking anything. What does it mean? | A few people came | How many people came? | No one came | Ten people came
test: «wǒ dì èr» says Ming after the race. What is his place? | Second | Two points | Twentieth | He has two legs
test: In a game, a kid writes «88» before logging off. What did he say? | Bye-bye | I'm 88 | I'm rich | I won 88 times
test: Ming's granny frowns at room 44 in a hotel. Why? | 4 sounds like «die», twice | 44 is too high up | 44 means «ten» | 44 is a lucky number
test: You try the tongue-twister fast and mix up sì and shí. Why is that so easy to do? | 4 and 10 sound alike to learners: sì and shí | Because 4 and 10 are written the same way | Because shí really means four in the south | Because nobody says sì
test: «thirty-one in chinese, quick» | sānshí yī | yī sānshí | sān yī shí | shí sān yī
exam: Identify the numeral denoted by the expression shí'èr. | Twelve | Twenty | Two | Two hundred
exam: Ascertain the principle by which Mandarin numerals between eleven and ninety-nine are composed. | Tens are named by a preceding digit, and units follow shí | Each numeral up to one hundred has an unrelated name | Units always precede the tens, as in «two-and-twenty» | Numerals are borrowed from English
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the numerical sequence 520. | Its pronunciation loosely resembles wǒ ài nǐ, used in jest about affection | It is the official emergency telephone number throughout the country | It signifies farewell in written chat | It denotes a perfect examination score
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the distinction between jǐ ge and duōshao? | The former suits small quantities, the latter larger or unknown amounts | The former concerns money exclusively, whereas the latter concerns persons | The two are interchangeable in every context without exception | The former denotes order, whereas the latter denotes age
